# ── EXAMPLE ONLY — the real file is GENERATED and is not in git ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `bin/box-up.sh` (step 3c) writes `coffee.caddy` next to this one, from the café store id that provisioning
# just returned. This copy exists so a reader can see the SHAPE without a running box; it is named `.example`
# so `caddy/Caddyfile.local`'s `import /etc/caddy/extra-local/*.caddy` does not pick it up.
#
# ⚠️ WHY IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN `caddy/extra/` (A10). This is a FRAGMENT — bare `handle` blocks, imported
# from INSIDE the bench's `:80` site block. `caddy/Caddyfile` imports `/etc/caddy/extra/*.caddy` at TOP level,
# where every file is parsed as a SITE BLOCK, and `*.caddy` MATCHES `coffee.local.caddy`: a `.local.` in the
# name is a strict subset of the wider glob, never an escape from it. Production therefore imported this
# fragment at top level and `caddy validate` answered `parsed 'handle' as a site address` — which is not a
# broken extra host but a DEAD EDGE, with the store, the checkout and the admin down together. One directory,
# one reader; `bin/caddy-extra-door.guard.mjs` holds it.
#
# ⚠️ WHY THE REAL ONE IS NOT VERSIONED, and it is the same defect this whole rule exists to prevent: the
# store id is a fresh ULID on every birth. A committed copy is correct for exactly one box and a LIE for
# every other — it routes to a store that does not exist, the request falls through to the vanilla
# storefront, and the page still looks right because the theme comes from the store. That is precisely the
# bug that hid the café's fork not serving the café, and committing a generated file re-creates it.
#
# <CAFE_STORE_ID> below is what the generator substitutes.

handle /s/<CAFE_STORE_ID>/checkout* {
	header X-Forge-Served-By "checkout"
	reverse_proxy checkout:3000
}
handle /s/<CAFE_STORE_ID>* {
	header X-Forge-Served-By "storefront-coffee"
	reverse_proxy storefront-coffee:3000
}
handle /s/cafe* {
	header X-Forge-Served-By "storefront-coffee"
	reverse_proxy storefront-coffee:3000
}
